{%- comment -%}
  Delivery estimate — "Arrives Wed, 15 – Mon, 20 Oct".

  Usage:
    {% render 'delivery-estimate' %}                  full line
    {% render 'delivery-estimate', compact: true %}   dates only, no icon

  Why it exists: the PDP said "ships within 48 hours" and the Shipping tab
  said "1–4 business days". Neither answers the question a shopper is
  actually asking, which is what day the bottle lands. 20% of shoppers who
  abandon a checkout give slow delivery as the reason, and they can only
  weigh that if they are told before the payment step rather than after it.

  ── The dates are real ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Worked out from theme settings — the dispatch cutoff, the fastest and
  slowest delivery in business days, and which days are not worked — against
  the store's own Shipping policy. Order after the cutoff and the clock
  starts the next business day, exactly as that policy says. Nothing here is
  a countdown or an invented promise; change the settings and this line
  changes with them.

  The working week is a setting and not a constant because this got it wrong
  once. It assumed Monday to Friday — the UAE federal working week — while
  the store's Shipping policy says "Handling time: typically 1 business day,
  Monday to Saturday". Treating Saturday as a rest day inserted a day that
  does not exist into every estimate, so each one promised delivery a day
  later than the business actually works to. The default below is Sunday
  only, which is what that policy describes.

  Note also that the policy's window is 1–4 business days "in total",
  handling included — so these are counted from the order, not from dispatch.

  ── Why it is also computed in JavaScript ────────────────────────────────

  Liquid renders the correct dates at render time, and Shopify caches pages.
  A PDP rendered last night and served this morning would quote yesterday's
  estimate with no way to tell. So the figures are also carried as data
  attributes and app.js recomputes them on load, correcting the line if the
  page came from cache. Server-rendered first means no layout shift and no
  blank slot, and the line still reads correctly with JavaScript off — it is
  only ever wrong by a cache's age, and only until JS runs.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign cutoff = settings.dispatch_cutoff_hour | default: 18 | times: 1
  assign days_min = settings.delivery_days_min | default: 1 | times: 1
  assign days_max = settings.delivery_days_max | default: 4 | times: 1

  assign now_hour = 'now' | date: '%H' | times: 1
  assign now_dow = 'now' | date: '%w' | times: 1
  assign now_ts = 'now' | date: '%s' | times: 1

  # Rest days as %w numbers: 0 is Sunday, 6 is Saturday. Comma-delimited on
  # both sides when tested below so "6" can never match inside "16".
  case settings.delivery_rest_days
    when 'fri'
      assign rest_days = '5'
    when 'fri-sat'
      assign rest_days = '5,6'
    when 'sat-sun'
      assign rest_days = '0,6'
    when 'none'
      assign rest_days = ''
    else
      assign rest_days = '0'
  endcase
  assign rest_key = ',' | append: rest_days | append: ','

  # Past the cutoff, nothing is picked today — the clock starts tomorrow.
  assign lead = 0
  if now_hour >= cutoff
    assign lead = 1
  endif

  # Walk forward a day at a time, counting only working days, until the
  # fastest and the slowest have both been reached. Liquid has no date
  # arithmetic, so the rest days have to be stepped over by hand.
  assign min_offset = 0
  assign max_offset = 0
  assign counted = 0
  for i in (1..40)
    assign cursor = lead | plus: i
    assign dow = now_dow | plus: cursor | modulo: 7
    assign dow_key = ',' | append: dow | append: ','
    if rest_key contains dow_key
      continue
    endif
    assign counted = counted | plus: 1
    if counted == days_min and min_offset == 0
      assign min_offset = cursor
    endif
    if counted >= days_max
      assign max_offset = cursor
      break
    endif
  endfor

  assign min_ts = min_offset | times: 86400 | plus: now_ts
  assign max_ts = max_offset | times: 86400 | plus: now_ts
  assign min_label = min_ts | date: '%a, %-d %b'
  assign max_label = max_ts | date: '%a, %-d %b'
-%}

<span class="delivery-est"
      data-delivery-estimate
      data-cutoff="{{ cutoff }}"
      data-days-min="{{ days_min }}"
      data-days-max="{{ days_max }}"
      data-rest-days="{{ rest_days }}">
  {%- unless compact -%}<span class="mi" aria-hidden="true">event_available</span>{%- endunless -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    One range, not two dates with a label each: "Arrives Wed, 15 – Mon, 20
    Oct" is read at a glance, where "Earliest … Latest …" asks the shopper to
    do the comparison. Collapsed to a single date when the two fall on the
    same day, which they do whenever fastest and slowest are set the same.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <span class="de-text">
    {%- if min_label == max_label -%}
      Arrives <b data-delivery-max>{{ max_label }}</b>
    {%- else -%}
      Arrives <b data-delivery-min>{{ min_label }}</b> – <b data-delivery-max>{{ max_label }}</b>
    {%- endif -%}
  </span>
</span>
